// 六级 8000 词 · 每组配套短文 freq.ext（由 build/passages/freq.ext.txt 生成）
CET6.passages("freq.ext",{"1":{"w":["according","cultural","online","tech","center","marketing","non","related","spending","creativity","lifestyle","wellbeing","educational","vegetarian","celebrity","labor","awareness","app","psychologist","diversity"],"title":"The Influencer Economy","t":"Ten years ago, few people had heard the word \"influencer\". Today, **according** to industry reports, companies spend billions of dollars each year on **online** advertising through social media stars. For many young people, becoming an internet **celebrity** is now a dream career.\n\nThe trend has changed **marketing**. Instead of paying for television advertisements, brands send products to influencers who share them on their favourite **app**. A **vegetarian** cook might promote a new plant-based burger, while a fitness star advertises sports clothes. Consumer **spending** on products **related** to influencers has grown rapidly, especially among people who see these stars as models for their own **lifestyle**.\n\nSupporters say the industry encourages **creativity** and **diversity**. People from many **cultural** backgrounds can build an audience without the help of traditional media. Some influencers produce **educational** content, teaching science, history or languages. Others raise **awareness** of social issues, from climate change to the rights of workers in the **tech** industry.\n\nBut there are concerns. One **psychologist** warns that constant comparison with perfect online images can harm young people's **wellbeing**. Influencers themselves often describe their work as exhausting **labor**: they must post every day, answer messages and keep their followers happy. Many burn out within a few years.\n\nSome governments now require influencers to label paid advertisements clearly. A **non**-profit research **center** in London has also published advice for parents. Its message is simple: enjoy social media, but remember that what you see is often a carefully designed performance, not real life."},"2":{"w":["judgment","gender","sales","dietary","nomad","fewer","loneliness","willpower","prioritize","sustainable","technological","esteem","inequality","aging","detrimental","meter","ecological","smartphone","teen","conducive"],"title":"The Digital Nomad Life","t":"Thanks to fast internet and **technological** change, a growing number of workers no longer need an office. They carry a laptop and a **smartphone**, and they work from beaches, mountain villages and city cafés. They call themselves digital **nomads|nomad**, and their numbers have tripled in a decade.\n\nThe lifestyle has obvious attractions. Living in cheaper countries means **fewer** bills and more free time. Many nomads say that travel improves their self-**esteem** and makes them more creative. Some even choose destinations that are **conducive** to healthy habits, such as places where fresh fruit is cheap and **dietary** choices are easy. One woman lives in a flat of just twenty square **meters|meter**, but says the sea view from her desk makes up for it.\n\nYet the reality can be harder. Many nomads report deep **loneliness**, especially when they move often and leave friends behind. Working alone requires strong **willpower**, and it can be difficult to **prioritize** tasks without a manager. Some experts believe that the constant travel may be **detrimental** to long-term health and relationships.\n\nThere are wider concerns too. Local residents in popular destinations complain that rising rents push them out of their own neighbourhoods, increasing **inequality**. Flights also have an **ecological** cost. Critics argue that the nomad lifestyle is not **sustainable** unless travellers stay longer and support local businesses. **Sales** of long-stay visas show that many countries see nomads as a source of income, but they also need clear rules.\n\nThe movement is changing in other ways. More women are joining, narrowing the **gender** gap that once defined it. Some older people now become nomads after retiring, a sign of how society's attitude to **aging** is shifting. Even a **teen** with an online job can join them. In the end, experts say, each traveller must make a personal **judgment** about whether the freedom is worth the cost."},"3":{"w":["neighbor","guideline","antibiotic","sarcasm","affordable","behavioral","renewable","bolster","crave","obesity","neuroscience","bilingual","cyberloafing","perfectionism","variability","medication","calorie","ultra","simulation","solitude"],"title":"The Science of Bad Habits","t":"Why do we check our phones at work, eat snacks we do not need or put off important tasks? A new field of **behavioral** research is trying to answer these questions, combining **neuroscience** with psychology.\n\nOne common habit has a modern name: **cyberloafing**, or using the internet for personal reasons during working hours. Studies show **variability** between workers, but most people do it for at least an hour a day. Researchers say that we **crave** small rewards, such as new messages, which release pleasant chemicals in the brain. In computer **simulations|simulation**, the brain's reward system behaves much like it does with food.\n\nFood habits are similar. Many offices offer cheap, **ultra**-processed snacks that are high in **calories|calorie** but low in nutrition, contributing to rising **obesity**. Experts recommend clear **guidelines|guideline**, such as offering fruit and water instead of sweets. Some companies have also invested in **affordable** healthy meals and **renewable** energy, hoping to **bolster** both health and image.\n\nNot every bad habit looks like laziness. **Perfectionism** can also harm productivity: people who fear mistakes often delay starting work. Some employees need **solitude** to concentrate, while others prefer to chat with a **neighbor** at the next desk. One researcher noted with gentle **sarcasm** that \"open-plan offices were designed by people who never had to think.\"\n\nDoctors warn that we often look for quick fixes. Just as patients sometimes demand an **antibiotic** for a cold, workers may hope that a pill or **medication** can improve concentration. The real solution, experts say, is slower: changing routines one step at a time. Interestingly, studies suggest that **bilingual** people may find it easier to switch between tasks, perhaps because they practise mental control every day."},"4":{"w":["dementia","passionate","cooperation","activate","hostility","recreate","enzyme","telomere","unexcused","yeah","creation","jargon","password","investor","biological","biodiversity","artifact","robotic","supervisor","dissertation"],"title":"A Dissertation on Ageing","t":"For four years, Maria Lopez has been working on her doctoral **dissertation** about the **biological** causes of ageing. Her research focuses on the **telomere**, a protective cap at the end of each chromosome. Each time a cell divides, its telomeres become shorter, and scientists believe this is one reason why our bodies age.\n\nMaria is **passionate** about her work. She studies an **enzyme** that can repair telomeres and tries to **activate** it in laboratory cells. If scientists can **recreate** this process safely in humans, it might one day help to slow diseases such as **dementia**. \"That's the dream,\" she says. \"**Yeah**, it sounds like science fiction, but every great **creation** starts as a crazy idea.\"\n\nHer work depends on **cooperation**. She shares data with laboratories in three countries, and her **supervisor** meets her every week. Much of the analysis is done by **robotic** machines that handle thousands of samples a day. The laboratory's computers are protected by complex security; Maria jokes that she has changed her **password** more times than she has changed her shoes.\n\nResearch is not always smooth. When she first presented her results, some scientists responded with **hostility**, questioning her methods. She has also learned to avoid technical **jargon** when explaining her work to the public. A private **investor** recently showed interest in funding further studies, which pleased her university.\n\nOutside the lab, Maria loves nature. On weekends, she volunteers at a museum, cataloguing an ancient **artifact** collection, and she helps with a local project to protect **biodiversity** in wetlands. She has never had an **unexcused** absence from either job. \"Science is about curiosity,\" she says. \"So is life.\""},"5":{"w":["archaeologist","innovative","mindset","policymaker","grid","mindfulness","seafood","haven","vegan","founder","scenario","socialize","cognition","restoration","advancement","equity","implementation","manufacturing","monolingual","motherhood"],"title":"A Town Rebuilds Itself","t":"When the fish factory closed in a small coastal town in Norway, many residents feared that the community would die. For decades, the factory had been the centre of local **manufacturing**, processing **seafood** for export. Without it, young families began to leave.\n\nThen a local **archaeologist** made a remarkable discovery: the remains of a Viking settlement near the harbour. The find attracted visitors from across Europe, and town leaders saw an opportunity. They began the **restoration** of old buildings and created a museum. One **policymaker** described the plan as \"turning history into a future\".\n\nThe town has taken an **innovative** approach. Its new businesses include a café run by a young **founder** who makes **vegan** versions of traditional fish dishes, and a small company connecting homes to a local solar energy **grid**. Planners considered every **scenario**, from a tourism boom to a sudden decline, and designed policies to support local residents. The careful **implementation** of these plans has impressed experts.\n\nThe project also focuses on people. The town offers free **mindfulness** classes for older residents and spaces where newcomers can **socialize**. Studies suggest that social contact protects **cognition** as people age. For many families, the town has become a **haven** from the stress of big cities. Organisers have tried to ensure **equity**, so that the benefits reach everyone.\n\nChange requires a new **mindset**. Many local children were once **monolingual**, speaking only Norwegian; now, with international visitors, they learn English and German at school. Young women say that the town's support for **motherhood**, including free childcare, has encouraged them to stay. Career **advancement** is still limited, but the town is no longer shrinking."},"6":{"w":["duckling","mid","devastate","laptop","distraction","habitat","opt","learner","unrelated","copyright","flavor","hectare","arbor","mortality","participation","align","coverage","designer","erode","frustration"],"title":"Citizens Count the Ducks","t":"Every spring, hundreds of volunteers gather at a wetland outside the city to count birds. The project began after floods in 2015 **devastated|devastate** the area, destroying nests and washing away soil. Today, the wetland covers more than two hundred **hectares|hectare**, and it has become one of the most important bird **habitats|habitat** in the region.\n\nAnyone can take part. Volunteers range from retired teachers to schoolchildren, and no experience is needed; each **learner** receives a short training session. Many **opt** to come early in the morning, when the birds are most active. In **mid**-May, the highlight is counting the young **ducklings|duckling** that follow their mothers across the water.\n\nThe data is valuable. Scientists use it to calculate bird **mortality** and to track how the wetland is changing. Rising water levels continue to **erode** the banks, and some species are declining. A local software **designer** created a simple app for recording sightings on a phone or **laptop**, which has greatly increased **participation**.\n\nThe project is not without **frustration**. Bad weather often cancels counts, and noisy visitors can be a **distraction** for both birds and volunteers. Some people photograph the birds and post the images online without permission, raising questions of **copyright**. The organisers ask everyone to **align** their behaviour with simple rules: stay on the paths and keep dogs on a lead.\n\nThe wetland has also received national media **coverage**. Every April, the city celebrates **Arbor** Day by planting trees along its edge. Local beekeepers say the new trees give their honey a special **flavor**. Even residents whose jobs are completely **unrelated** to nature now feel that the wetland belongs to them."},"7":{"w":["innovate","irrelevant","navigate","pesticide","prestigious","entrepreneurial","honor","ongoing","partnership","uncertainty","individualism","selective","stressful","beverage","chimpanzee","factual","perfectionist","decease","sarcastic","acclaim"],"title":"The Juice That Won an Award","t":"Three years ago, a young woman named Emily Zhang left her job at a bank to start a business making organic fruit drinks. Her friends thought it was a risky decision. Last month, her company won a **prestigious** national award for small businesses, and the **acclaim** has changed her life.\n\nEmily has always been **entrepreneurial**. As a teenager, she sold homemade lemonade at markets. When she noticed that most **beverage** companies used fruit grown with large amounts of **pesticide**, she decided to **innovate**. She formed a **partnership** with local farmers who grow fruit without chemicals and pay fair wages to workers.\n\nRunning a business has not been easy. Emily describes the early years as **stressful**, full of **uncertainty** about money and supplies. She admits she is a **perfectionist**; she once threw away a whole batch of juice because the colour was slightly wrong. Learning to **navigate** regulations and contracts took time. Some investors gave **sarcastic** comments, telling her that organic drinks were a passing trend.\n\nEmily's inspiration came from an unexpected source: a documentary about a scientist who studied the **chimpanzee** in African forests. The scientist's patience and respect for nature moved her. Emily is **selective** about her own partners and rejects companies that ignore environmental rules. She believes that modern **individualism** has made people forget their responsibility to the community.\n\nIn her award speech, Emily paid tribute to her grandfather, who had encouraged her before his **decease** the previous year. \"It is an **honor** to receive this,\" she said. \"But the real work is **ongoing**.\" She ended with a **factual** reminder: questions about price alone are **irrelevant** if the planet cannot produce food in the future."},"8":{"w":["assortment","glamorous","intuitive","supporter","genre","livelihood","majestic","outperform","supportive","sustainability","autonomy","blog","indicator","individualistic","carbohydrate","cosmetic","defer","gadget","nutritional","socioeconomic"],"title":"A Food Blog from the Mountains","t":"When Ana started her food **blog** five years ago, she wrote about restaurants in the city. Then she moved to a small mountain village, and her writing changed completely. Today, her blog describes the traditional food of farming families, and it has thousands of readers.\n\nThe mountains are **majestic**, but life there is hard. Most families earn their **livelihood** from farming and sheep, and incomes are low. Ana's posts describe simple dishes, such as bread, cheese and potato soup, and explain their **nutritional** value. She notes that the local diet is high in **carbohydrate** but balanced with fresh vegetables and milk.\n\nAna's writing style is warm and **intuitive**. She avoids the **glamorous** photos common in the food **genre**, preferring natural pictures of real kitchens. Readers say her blog makes them feel at home. She does not care about **cosmetic** perfection; a cracked plate or a messy table is part of the story.\n\nHer work has wider significance. Villagers once felt that young people saw their way of life as old-fashioned. Ana's blog has shown that traditional food can **outperform** modern fast food in both taste and health, and it has become a useful **indicator** of growing interest in rural life. Researchers studying the **socioeconomic** future of mountain villages have cited her work.\n\nThe village has been **supportive** of her project. Older women share recipes, and some even **defer** to her judgment on new dishes. One neighbour gave her an **assortment** of homemade jams. Ana values her **autonomy** as a writer, and she rejects offers to promote kitchen **gadgets|gadget**. She sees herself not as an **individualistic** artist but as a **supporter** of the community. Her goal is **sustainability**: \"If these traditions survive, the mountains survive.\""},"9":{"w":["viable","vitality","islander","multinational","surf","workout","couplet","unsold","developer","healthcare","interpersonal","irrational","outweigh","reshape","catastrophic","economist","endorse","fabulous","individuality","industrialization"],"title":"Paradise for Sale?","t":"On a small island in the Pacific, a **multinational** company wants to build a luxury resort. The plan includes hotels, a golf course and a harbour for yachts. The company's **developer** promises jobs and investment, and some local leaders **endorse** the idea. But many islanders are worried.\n\nSupporters argue that the project is economically **viable**. The island has few jobs, and young people often leave for the mainland. A resort would bring money for schools and **healthcare**, and tourists would come to **surf** and enjoy the beaches. \"The benefits **outweigh** the costs,\" one local **economist** said. \"Without growth, the island will lose its **vitality**.\"\n\nOpponents disagree. They fear that rapid **industrialization** of the coast could be **catastrophic** for coral reefs and fishing. Many **islanders|islander** also worry about their culture. Traditional life is based on close **interpersonal** relationships, and they fear that mass tourism will **reshape** the community. \"We don't want to lose our **individuality**,\" one teacher said.\n\nThe debate has become emotional. At one public meeting, a man called the opposition **irrational**, while a woman replied that money cannot buy happiness. An elderly fisherman recited a traditional **couplet** about the sea, which received loud applause.\n\nSome experts suggest a middle path: smaller, locally owned guesthouses rather than one giant resort. Tourists, they say, increasingly prefer authentic experiences to **fabulous** hotels. Several resorts on nearby islands already have **unsold** villas. Visitors who come for a morning **workout** on the beach and a simple dinner with local families may be the island's best future."},"10":{"w":["ineffective","isolation","misconception","personalize","problematic","regional","scary","validate","addiction","advertiser","alienate","archive","collaborative","depiction","emulate","exclusion","maximize","worsen","diffusion","experimenter"],"title":"Rethinking Phone Addiction","t":"Many people worry that they spend too much time on their phones. Newspapers often warn of a smartphone **addiction**, and parents fear that screens are damaging their children. But a growing number of researchers argue that the problem is more complicated.\n\nOne common **misconception**, they say, is that all screen time is harmful. In fact, much depends on how phones are used. Video calls can reduce **isolation**, especially for people living far from family. **Collaborative** online projects can help students learn. Simply counting hours may be **ineffective** as a way of measuring harm.\n\nStill, some aspects of phone use are **problematic**. Apps are designed to **maximize** the time users spend on them, and **advertisers|advertiser** pay to **personalize** the content each user sees. Critics say this can **worsen** anxiety and **alienate** people from those around them. The **depiction** of perfect lives online leads some young people to **emulate** unrealistic standards, and those who cannot keep up may feel a sense of **exclusion**.\n\nScientists are trying to **validate** these claims. In one study, an **experimenter** asked volunteers to give up social media for a month. The results were mixed: some felt happier, but others felt lonely. Researchers also study the **diffusion** of trends, examining how ideas spread across **regional** and national networks. Some findings are **scary**; false news can travel faster than true stories.\n\nTo support future research, one university has created a digital **archive** of social media posts from the past twenty years. Experts hope it will help us understand how technology has changed society, and how we can build a healthier relationship with it."},"11":{"w":["haze","levy","marketer","onset","predator","aspirational","comet","diplomat","doodle","drone","inbox","loess","motivator","offender","reforestation","riches","spire","deplete","openness","societal"],"title":"Greening the Loess Plateau","t":"The **Loess** Plateau in northern China was once one of the most eroded places on Earth. Centuries of farming had **depleted|deplete** the soil, and every rainy season, tonnes of yellow earth washed into the rivers. Dust storms and **haze** regularly covered nearby cities.\n\nOver the past thirty years, one of the world's largest **reforestation** projects has transformed the region. Millions of trees and shrubs have been planted on the hillsides, and farmers have built terraces to hold the soil. Today, workers use a **drone** to drop seeds onto steep slopes that people cannot reach. The **onset** of the rainy season no longer brings disaster.\n\nThe project has had wide **societal** effects. Incomes have risen, and many families who once lived in poverty now earn money from fruit orchards. The government also introduced a **levy** on polluting factories, and any repeat **offender** faces heavy fines. With cleaner air, villagers say they can see the night sky clearly; some even watched a bright **comet** pass last winter.\n\nNature has returned too. Small animals are back, and so are the **predators|predator** that hunt them, such as foxes and eagles. Scientists see this as a sign of a healthy ecosystem. Foreign **diplomats|diplomat** and environmental groups now visit to learn from the project, and officials have shown a new **openness** to sharing data.\n\nFor young people, the green hills are a powerful **motivator**. Children **doodle** trees in their notebooks, and a local **marketer** has built a brand around the region's apples, presenting them as an **aspirational** product. One farmer, checking orders in her email **inbox**, says the land has given her **riches** she never expected. From the hilltop, she can see the **spire** of the old village temple rising above a sea of green."},"12":{"w":["traveling","unrealistic","anticipation","aspire","assertion","compassion","constructive","demographic","deter","diplomacy","enthusiast","environmentalist","feat","flip","halfway","indigenous","lifespan","mandatory","manipulative","milestone"],"title":"Walking Across a Continent","t":"Last year, a thirty-year-old **environmentalist** named Lucy Harper set out to walk from the north of South America to its southern tip. Friends called the plan **unrealistic**; the journey was more than eight thousand kilometres long. But Lucy had long **aspired|aspire** to see the continent's forests and rivers on foot.\n\n**Traveling** alone, she carried only a small tent and a solar phone charger. Her shoes had a short **lifespan**; she wore out six pairs. When she reached the **halfway** point in Bolivia, she celebrated the **milestone** with local farmers, who called her walk an extraordinary **feat**. Her online followers watched each update with growing **anticipation**.\n\nAlong the way, Lucy met many **indigenous** communities. She learned that their traditional knowledge protects the forests, and she was moved by their **compassion** for strangers. She also saw how logging and mining were destroying land. Some companies, she said, used **manipulative** advertising to hide the damage. She rejected the **assertion** that development and conservation cannot go together.\n\nLucy's walk required careful **diplomacy**. In some regions, **mandatory** permits were needed to enter protected areas, and she spent days negotiating with officials. She tried to keep her criticism **constructive**, offering ideas rather than simply attacking governments. Danger did not **deter** her, though she admits that she sometimes had to **flip** her plans at the last minute.\n\nLucy's journey attracted attention from every **demographic**, from schoolchildren to retired engineers. One nature **enthusiast** from Chile walked with her for two weeks. \"I wanted to show that one person can make a difference,\" Lucy says. \"But the real heroes are the people who live there.\""},"13":{"w":["outright","perseverance","respondent","scrutinize","uncontrollable","urbanization","adherence","afterwards","conditioner","conscientious","dependence","diabetes","expectancy","idol","immersive","inborn","infringe","interviewer","lawmaker","mandate"],"title":"Sugar, Cities and Health","t":"**Diabetes** is spreading rapidly around the world. Experts link the rise to **urbanization**: as people move to cities, they exercise less and eat more processed food. In some countries, the disease now threatens to reduce life **expectancy** for the first time in decades.\n\nA recent survey tried to understand people's habits. Each **interviewer** asked questions about diet, exercise and stress. Many a **respondent** admitted an almost **uncontrollable** craving for sweet drinks and a **dependence** on fast food. Researchers say that some people have an **inborn** preference for sugar, but that habits matter more. Even **conscientious** people find it hard to change.\n\nDoctors stress the importance of treatment **adherence**: patients must take their medicine and follow a healthy diet consistently. This requires **perseverance**, especially when results are slow. New technology may help; some hospitals offer **immersive** virtual-reality exercise games that make workouts more enjoyable. Patients often feel better **afterwards**, and some continue at home.\n\nGovernments face difficult choices. One **lawmaker** has proposed an **outright** ban on sugary drink advertising aimed at children, especially adverts that feature a famous pop **idol**. Critics say such laws **infringe** on personal freedom. Others argue that the state has a **mandate** to protect public health, as it does with tobacco.\n\nExperts urge the public to **scrutinize** food labels carefully. Many products that seem healthy contain hidden sugar. Simple changes, such as walking instead of driving, can make a big difference. As one doctor said, sitting all day in an office with the air **conditioner** on is \"the most dangerous job in the modern world\"."},"14":{"w":["meticulous","needy","perpetuate","predictable","preoccupy","proofread","rationality","relaxation","responsive","seeker","tenure","theorist","unpredictable","anthropologist","anti","bilingualism","civic","colorful","consultation","eater"],"title":"An Anthropologist in a Bilingual Town","t":"For three years, the **anthropologist** Dr. Elena Ruiz lived in a small town on the border between Spain and France. She wanted to study **bilingualism**: how people who use two languages every day think about identity, family and community. Her book on the town has just been published.\n\nDr. Ruiz is famous for being **meticulous**. She recorded thousands of conversations and wrote detailed notes every evening. She even asked a friend to **proofread** each chapter twice. She found that most residents switch languages in **predictable** ways: one language at home, another at work. Yet some patterns were surprisingly **unpredictable**, changing with mood or topic.\n\nThe town has a rich **civic** life. Residents meet regularly at public **consultation** events, where the local council asks for their views. The council is unusually **responsive**, and many people say they feel listened to. The town is also famous for its **colorful** festivals, which celebrate both cultures and help to **perpetuate** old traditions.\n\nNot everything is harmonious. Some residents worry about tourism, and a small **anti**-tourism group has formed. Rising rents put pressure on **needy** families. Questions of **rationality** versus tradition often **preoccupy** local debates, such as whether to modernise the old market. Dr. Ruiz notes that every community is a **seeker** of balance between change and continuity.\n\nHer work has impressed scholars. A leading **theorist** of language praised the book, and the university recently granted Dr. Ruiz **tenure**. She says her favourite memories are simple: long lunches, cheerful arguments and her neighbour, a famously fussy **eater**, refusing to try a new dish. For **relaxation**, she still visits the town every summer."},"15":{"w":["folklore","fulfillment","giver","infection","pizza","rethink","specialty","unaffordable","blender","diner","domesticate","hazy","houseplant","phonic","sleeper","surveillance","syndrome","teenage","accumulation","competitiveness"],"title":"The Little Diner on Main Street","t":"For twenty years, Rosa Martinez has run a small **diner** on the main street of her town. Her **specialty** is homemade **pizza**, cooked in a wood-fired oven, and every morning she makes fresh fruit smoothies in an old **blender**. Customers come from miles away.\n\nThe diner has survived many challenges. Rising rents have made many shops in town **unaffordable**, and big restaurant chains have greater **competitiveness**. Rosa has had to **rethink** her business more than once. During a wave of **infection** a few years ago, she was forced to close for months, and the **accumulation** of unpaid bills nearly ruined her.\n\nRosa is a natural **giver**. She feeds homeless people for free, and she hires **teenage** students from the local school. One boy, who suffers from a rare **syndrome** that affects his speech, practises **phonic** reading exercises with her between orders. \"Work should bring **fulfillment**, not just money,\" she says.\n\nThe diner itself is full of character. Every table has a small **houseplant**, and the walls are covered with stories from local **folklore**, such as the legend of a farmer who tried to **domesticate** a wild wolf. On **hazy** autumn mornings, passengers from the overnight **sleeper** train come in for coffee before continuing their journey.\n\nModern life has brought some changes. Rosa installed a **surveillance** camera after a break-in last year, though she dislikes it. \"Trust is the most important ingredient,\" she says. \"The camera is just for the insurance company.\""},"16":{"w":["credible","holder","involvement","opposition","thorny","unfamiliar","activist","adulthood","animation","categorize","cornerstone","culturally","differentiate","ethic","legislature","metaphor","micro","paradigm","pro","prosper"],"title":"A Young Activist Changes the Law","t":"At seventeen, Sofia Reyes became one of the youngest people ever to speak before her country's **legislature**. She had spent two years campaigning for a law to protect small family farms, and her story shows how young people can influence politics long before **adulthood**.\n\nSofia's **involvement** began in school, when she noticed that farmers in her region were struggling to survive against large companies. She started making short **animation** videos explaining the problem and posted them online. The videos were simple but **credible**, using real data and interviews. Within months, she had become a well-known **activist**.\n\nThe issue was **thorny**. Supporters argued that family farms are the **cornerstone** of rural life and that they help local economies **prosper**. **Opposition** came from businesses that claimed the law would raise food prices. Sofia carefully weighed the **pros|pro** and cons in her speeches and tried to **differentiate** between large farms and small **micro**-businesses that could not compete.\n\nPoliticians were often **unfamiliar** with the details, so Sofia learned to **categorize** her arguments clearly. She used a **metaphor** that became famous: \"Our farms are the roots of the tree. Cut them, and the tree falls.\" She also spoke about the strong work **ethic** of farming families and the **culturally** important traditions they protect.\n\nThe law finally passed last spring. Experts described it as a **paradigm** shift in agricultural policy. Sofia was invited to meet the President, and she became the **holder** of a national youth award. Asked about her future, she smiled. \"First,\" she said, \"I need to finish my exams.\""},"17":{"w":["reminder","reportedly","reproductive","risky","sidewalk","simplistic","sinful","sooner","supposedly","talented","teammate","tricky","unclear","unprocessed","unwillingness","vaccine","adaptability","aesthetics","arousal","auction"],"title":"The Charity Auction","t":"Last month, a small art gallery in our city held a charity **auction** to raise money for a children's hospital. The star of the evening was a **talented** nineteen-year-old painter named Leo Park, whose work had **reportedly** attracted interest from collectors abroad.\n\nLeo's path to art was unusual. He had been a promising footballer until an injury ended his career. One of his former **teammates|teammate** encouraged him to try painting. At first, he drew pictures on the **sidewalk** with chalk, and people stopped to watch. His **adaptability**, he says, came from sport: \"When one door closes, you learn to run in a different direction.\"\n\nThe auction raised more money than expected. Funds will support a **vaccine** programme for children in poor countries and a clinic offering **reproductive** health services to young mothers. Organisers said they would announce exact figures **sooner** than planned because the result was so good.\n\nNot everything went smoothly. Pricing art is **tricky**, and some guests showed an **unwillingness** to bid at first. It was **unclear** whether Leo's paintings would sell at all. Some critics called his work **simplistic**, but others praised its bold **aesthetics**. A psychologist in the audience explained that bright colours increase emotional **arousal**, which may be why people respond so strongly to his paintings.\n\nAt dinner, guests enjoyed a menu of **unprocessed**, locally grown food, followed by a chocolate cake so rich that one guest called it **sinful**. Investing in young artists is **risky**, but supporters say it is worth it. Leo's final painting, **supposedly** inspired by his hospital stay as a boy, sold for the highest price. It serves as a **reminder**, he said, that hope can grow from hardship."},"18":{"w":["aversion","avoidance","breach","clog","condemnation","congestion","conservationist","counterproductive","democrat","entitlement","escalate","exploratory","frontline","globalization","gut","iconic","interactive","mindlessly","neuroscientist","parental"],"title":"The Battle Over a Congestion Charge","t":"Like many American cities, our city suffers from terrible traffic **congestion**. Every morning, cars **clog** the main roads, and a trip that should take ten minutes can last an hour. Last year, the mayor proposed a charge for drivers entering the city centre during rush hour. The debate that followed was fierce.\n\nSupporters, including many local **Democrat** politicians and a well-known **conservationist** group, argued that the charge would reduce pollution and fund public transport. An **exploratory** study suggested that traffic could fall by a fifth. A **neuroscientist** from the local university explained that long, stressful commutes affect the brain, increasing anxiety and anger. **Parental** groups added that cleaner air would protect children near schools.\n\nOpponents reacted with strong **condemnation**. Some drivers felt a sense of **entitlement** to use public roads freely, and others showed a deep **aversion** to any new tax. Business owners warned that the charge would be **counterproductive**, driving shoppers away. As the argument began to **escalate**, critics accused the mayor of a **breach** of his election promises.\n\nThe city tried to calm the debate. It created an **interactive** online map showing how the charge would work, and officials listened to **frontline** workers, such as nurses and delivery drivers, who need to travel at rush hour. Many residents admitted they had never thought about the issue; they had simply driven **mindlessly** every day, out of habit.\n\nIn the end, the council approved a trial. The mayor said that **avoidance** was no longer an option: ignoring the problem had only made it worse. As cities around the world face the pressures of **globalization** and growth, many see the trial as an **iconic** test case. \"My **gut** tells me it will work,\" the mayor said."},"19":{"w":["planner","pleasurable","pollutant","preschool","preschooler","purist","recharge","reliant","rental","reputable","slump","solicitor","stifle","sub","symbolize","bracket","builder","chihuahua","enlightenment","glacier"],"title":"A New Life Near the Glacier","t":"Last year, our family moved from a busy city to a small town in the mountains, close to a huge **glacier**. My parents wanted a quieter life, and they hoped the clean air, free of the **pollutants|pollutant** we breathed in the city, would be good for my little sister's health.\n\nThe move was complicated. We found a **rental** house through a **reputable** agency, but the contract was confusing, so we hired a local **solicitor** to check it. A **builder** repaired the roof before we arrived. House prices in the town had suffered a **slump**, which made it affordable for families in our income **bracket**.\n\nMy sister, a four-year-old **preschooler**, started at the local **preschool**, where she quickly made friends. Our tiny **chihuahua**, who hated city traffic, now runs happily through the fields. My father, a city **planner**, works remotely, and he says the mountains help him **recharge**. Life is less **reliant** on cars; we walk almost everywhere.\n\nNot everything is perfect. Some newcomers say small-town life can **stifle** ambition, since there are few jobs. When my teacher was ill, a **sub** from the next valley had to drive an hour on icy roads. My mother, a coffee **purist**, complains that no café makes coffee the way she likes it.\n\nStill, living here is deeply **pleasurable**. Every morning, we see the glacier shining in the sun. For the town, it seems to **symbolize** both beauty and change, since it shrinks a little every year. My father calls our move a kind of **enlightenment**: \"We learned how little we really need.\""},"20":{"w":["implant","inconsistency","nutritious","parity","quitter","roommate","wearer","adaptive","agitate","ailment","alertness","arguable","astronomical","bother","brew","buffer","colonize","complementary","countless","dedication"],"title":"The Roommate Who Builds Gadgets","t":"My university **roommate**, Daniel, is an engineering student who is always building something. Our small flat is full of wires, circuit boards and **countless** half-finished projects. His latest invention is a smart wristband that monitors the **wearer**'s health.\n\nThe device measures heart rate, sleep and **alertness**, and it warns users when they show signs of a common **ailment**, such as dehydration. Daniel says it uses **adaptive** software that learns each person's habits. He hopes that in the future, similar devices could work alongside a medical **implant**, giving doctors real-time information.\n\nBuilding it has not been easy. Daniel found an **inconsistency** in his early data: the wristband gave different readings on different days. Solving the problem required great **dedication**. He worked late every night, stopping only to **brew** coffee and eat a quick, **nutritious** meal. When I suggested he take a break, he said he was no **quitter**.\n\nDaniel thinks about the wider picture too. He believes that health technology should be **complementary** to doctors, not a replacement. It is **arguable**, he says, whether devices can ever understand people as well as humans can. He also worries about cost: some medical gadgets are sold at **astronomical** prices, while engineers in poorer countries are paid far less. He supports pay **parity** across the industry.\n\nSometimes his enthusiasm can **agitate** our neighbours, especially when his machines beep at midnight. But I try not to let it **bother** me. His work has even taught me some science: he explained that bacteria **colonize** surfaces quickly, so his wristband has a special coating that acts as a **buffer** against germs. I suspect that one day he will be famous."},"21":{"w":["dual","dub","emergence","enact","farmland","functional","inevitability","insecurity","institutional","investigator","landmark","lavish","legislative","loom","marketplace","noteworthy","observational","originality","outdate","overburden"],"title":"Farms in the Sky","t":"As cities grow, they swallow the **farmland** around them. Experts warn that a food crisis may **loom** if this continues, and many countries already face rising food **insecurity**. One response has been the **emergence** of vertical farms: tall buildings where vegetables grow indoors under artificial light.\n\nThe idea is not new, but the technology has improved. Newspapers have **dubbed|dub** one farm in Singapore \"the farm in the sky\". Its thirty floors produce lettuce, herbs and strawberries for the local **marketplace**, using far less water than traditional farms. The building plays a **dual** role: it is both a farm and a tourist attraction, and it has become a city **landmark**.\n\nResearchers are studying whether such farms really work. One **investigator** led an **observational** study of ten vertical farms over three years. Her findings were **noteworthy**: yields were high, but energy costs were enormous. Some farms were **functional** only because of **lavish** government support, while others were funded by **institutional** investors hoping for future profits.\n\nGovernments are responding. Several countries have begun **legislative** work to support urban agriculture, and last year one city chose to **enact** a **landmark** law requiring new office buildings to include green roofs. Critics argue that old planning rules **outdate** quickly and that cities should not **overburden** builders with new requirements.\n\nExperts admit there is a certain **inevitability** to the trend. Land is limited, and populations are growing. The **originality** of vertical farming, they say, lies not in replacing traditional farms but in supporting them, bringing fresh food closer to the people who need it."},"22":{"w":["parenting","philosophical","plight","populate","reconstruct","regulatory","reintroduce","relentless","scenic","slash","sprawl","trustworthy","undeveloped","vulnerability","weird","accountable","accusation","aisle","akin","arena"],"title":"Bringing Back the Beaver","t":"Four hundred years ago, beavers lived in rivers across Britain. Then they were hunted to extinction for their fur. Now conservationists are trying to **reintroduce** them, and the results are both exciting and controversial.\n\nThe project began in a **scenic**, largely **undeveloped** valley in the west of England. Scientists released a few families of beavers and watched them closely. The animals were **relentless** builders, cutting down small trees and constructing dams. Within a few years, they had helped to **reconstruct** wetlands that had disappeared centuries ago. Fish and birds soon returned to **populate** the new pools.\n\nBeavers are fascinating animals. Their **parenting** is surprisingly careful: young beavers stay with their families for two years. Watching them, one scientist said, can make you **philosophical** about how humans and nature share the land. Some of their habits seem **weird**; they eat bark and store branches underwater for winter.\n\nBut not everyone is happy. Some farmers say the dams flood their fields, and they have made the **accusation** that scientists ignored their **plight**. They want someone to be held **accountable** for the damage. The issue has entered the political **arena**, and **regulatory** agencies are now reviewing the rules. In one supermarket, a farmer even put up a protest sign in the fruit **aisle**.\n\nSupporters argue that the benefits outweigh the costs. Beaver dams reduce flooding downstream, which matters more and more as urban **sprawl** covers land that once absorbed rain, and they improve water quality. Scientists insist their data is **trustworthy**. Still, the project reveals the **vulnerability** of any attempt to restore nature: it is **akin** to rebuilding a house while people are living in it. Some politicians have even threatened to **slash** funding for further releases."},"23":{"w":["awaken","backyard","behaviorally","biologist","breastfeed","canyon","capitalize","cheeseburger","commuter","compassionate","contagious","crumble","curtail","dataset","detachment","developmental","diagnosis","digestive","digitization","dominance"],"title":"What Babies Teach Scientists","t":"When the **biologist** Dr. Hannah Lee had her first child, she noticed something surprising: she could not stop observing her baby like a research subject. That curiosity led to a new project on early childhood **developmental** health, now one of the largest studies of its kind.\n\nThe project uses a **dataset** of more than fifty thousand children. The **digitization** of old hospital records allowed researchers to follow children from birth to adulthood. They examined how factors such as diet and sleep affect growth. One finding was clear: babies whose mothers **breastfeed** them tend to have healthier **digestive** systems in their first year.\n\nThe study also examines how children grow **behaviorally**. Hannah found that laughter is highly **contagious** among babies; one giggle can spread across a whole room. Children also show early signs of hand **dominance**, with most preferring their right hand by age two. Such findings can help doctors make an early **diagnosis** of developmental problems.\n\nHannah is a **compassionate** scientist. She believes researchers must balance professional **detachment** with respect for families. She also hopes her work will **awaken** parents to small daily choices that matter. \"You don't need expensive toys,\" she says. \"A **backyard** and some attention are enough.\"\n\nLike many working parents, Hannah is a daily **commuter**, and she admits to eating a **cheeseburger** in the car more often than she would like. Her research budget has been **curtailed|curtail** twice, and she worries that her project could **crumble** without new funding. On a recent family holiday to a desert **canyon**, she found new energy. Now she hopes to **capitalize** on public interest to secure support for the next ten years."},"24":{"w":["dweller","dysfunction","empower","environmentalism","evade","feasibility","harass","harassment","hmm","homeless","infringement","innovator","inspirational","integration","irrationally","lenient","longitudinal","lyric","maize","optimize"],"title":"A Home for Everyone","t":"In many big cities, the number of **homeless** people has risen sharply in recent years. For city **dwellers|dweller** walking past tents on the street, the problem is impossible to ignore. Experts say it reflects a deeper **dysfunction** in housing markets, where rents have grown far faster than wages.\n\nOne city has tried a new approach. Instead of offering temporary shelters, it gives homeless people a permanent flat first and then provides support with health and work. A **feasibility** study suggested the plan would save money in the long run, and a **longitudinal** study now follows participants for five years. Early results are **inspirational**: most people have kept their homes, and many have found jobs.\n\nThe programme aims to **empower** people rather than simply help them. Social workers support their **integration** into the community, encouraging them to join local projects. One **innovator** set up a garden where former homeless residents grow vegetables, including **maize** and beans. Another wrote a song about her experience; its **lyric** \"a door that opens\" became a local slogan.\n\nNot everyone supports the policy. Some residents argue that it is too **lenient** and rewards people who **evade** responsibility. Others react **irrationally** to the idea of new neighbours. Volunteers have faced online **harassment**, and a few critics have tried to **harass** staff at public meetings. A newspaper was also accused of copyright **infringement** after printing the song without permission.\n\nSupporters see the programme as part of a wider movement, linking social justice with **environmentalism** through energy-efficient housing. Officials continue to **optimize** the scheme. Asked whether it would work in other cities, one official paused. \"**Hmm**,\" she said. \"If they have the will, yes.\""},"25":{"w":["overwork","pharmacy","recreational","rectification","reexamine","regain","renowned","repetitive","rhetoric","scanner","spa","spokesperson","subsidize","sustainably","unaware","uncontrolled","uncritical","underrepresented","unfavorable","unintentionally"],"title":"Too Much Work?","t":"In many countries, long working hours have become normal. Employees answer emails late at night and rarely take holidays. Doctors warn that **overwork** can lead to serious health problems, including heart disease and depression. Many workers are **unaware** of the damage until it is too late.\n\nA **renowned** psychologist recently used a brain **scanner** to study tired workers. She found that people who perform **repetitive** tasks for long hours show reduced activity in areas linked to creativity. Another study found that stressed workers buy more sleep medicine at the **pharmacy**. Workers who take regular **recreational** breaks, on the other hand, **regain** energy quickly.\n\nSome companies have started to **reexamine** their policies. One technology firm now closes its offices at six o'clock and offers free visits to a local **spa**. Its **spokesperson** said that productivity had actually increased. Some governments **subsidize** such programmes, hoping to reduce health costs. Experts call for the **rectification** of outdated labour laws that allow **uncontrolled** overtime.\n\nCritics warn against **uncritical** acceptance of company claims. Some firms use the **rhetoric** of \"wellbeing\" without making real changes. Women and older workers remain **underrepresented** in senior roles, partly because long hours make it hard to balance work with family. Many of these problems were created **unintentionally**, as companies competed in difficult markets.\n\nExperts agree that change is needed. Working **sustainably**, they say, benefits both people and businesses. Despite **unfavorable** economic conditions, more workers are demanding shorter weeks. As one manager put it, \"Rest is not a reward. It is part of the job.\""},"26":{"w":["unleash","unused","unwanted","urgency","worldview","worthiness","algorithm","alleviation","bloody","burger","calligraphy","catwalk","childbearing","chore","clause","collectivistic","culinary","dependable","doze","e-commerce"],"title":"Shopping by Algorithm","t":"Online shopping has transformed daily life. **E-commerce** platforms now deliver almost anything within a day, from fashion inspired by the latest **catwalk** shows to **culinary** kits for home cooks and hot **burgers|burger** from local restaurants. For many busy families, shopping is no longer a **chore**.\n\nBehind every platform lies an **algorithm** that studies our behaviour and recommends products. These systems are designed to **unleash** our desire to buy. Flash sales create a sense of **urgency**, and small print in the terms and conditions hides important **clauses|clause** about returns. As a result, many people buy **unwanted** items, and their wardrobes fill with **unused** clothes.\n\nShopping habits also reflect culture. In more **collectivistic** societies, such as China, group buying is popular: friends and neighbours order together to get lower prices. Platforms also help rural sellers reach national markets, contributing to the **alleviation** of poverty. Craftspeople now sell traditional **calligraphy** brushes to customers across the world.\n\nResearchers have studied who buys what. Young parents in their **childbearing** years spend the most on baby products, while older shoppers value a **dependable** delivery service above low prices. One British shopper complained to a newspaper that delivery fees had become \"**bloody** ridiculous\". Others admit they **doze** in front of their screens late at night, scrolling through products they do not need.\n\nCritics argue that endless consumption shapes our **worldview**, teaching us that happiness comes from buying. They urge consumers to question the **worthiness** of each purchase. The next time an app suggests something new, they say, ask a simple question: do I really need it?"},"27":{"w":["electrify","euro","exclusivity","generational","high-speed","hydroelectric","impersonal","inequity","interviewee","introvert","irritation","migration","mishandle","morale","organisational","pandemic","patriotism","placement","plagiarism","planetary"],"title":"Europe's New Railways","t":"Across Europe, governments are investing billions of **euros|euro** in **high-speed** railways. The goal is to replace short flights with fast, clean trains. Many new lines are powered by **hydroelectric** energy from mountain rivers, and some countries plan to **electrify** every major line by 2040.\n\nThe plans gained support after the **pandemic**, when air travel collapsed and many people rediscovered trains. Surveys show strong **generational** differences: younger travellers care more about climate change and are happy to spend longer on the journey. One **interviewee**, a student on a work **placement** in Paris, said she had not flown for three years.\n\nRailways also connect communities. Better links make **migration** for work easier, and they help to reduce regional **inequity** by bringing jobs to poorer areas. Some politicians even link railways to **patriotism**, presenting them as symbols of national pride. Environmental groups prefer to stress the **planetary** benefits of lower emissions.\n\nNot everything has gone smoothly. One national railway was accused of trying to **mishandle** a major contract, and a report it published was later withdrawn over claims of **plagiarism**. Delays and cancellations cause **irritation** among passengers, and staff **morale** has suffered after years of **organisational** change.\n\nTravellers' experiences differ. Some find large modern stations **impersonal**, while an **introvert** may enjoy the quiet of a long train journey. Luxury trains promise **exclusivity**, with private cabins and fine dining. For most people, however, the main attraction is simple: city centre to city centre, with no airport queues."},"28":{"w":["plasticity","radiance","recruiter","rum","staffer","staple","tactic","therapist","tribal","unsolicited","ventilation","visionary","withdrawal","abound","accessibility","activation","administrator","advent","adviser","advisor"],"title":"Rewiring the Brain","t":"When Peter Moss suffered a stroke at the age of sixty, he could no longer speak clearly or walk without help. Doctors told his family that recovery would be slow. Two years later, Peter is walking again and talking with friends. His story shows the remarkable **plasticity** of the human brain: its ability to rebuild connections after damage.\n\nPeter's recovery took place at a rehabilitation centre founded by a **visionary** doctor in the 1990s. With the **advent** of new technology, the centre now uses brain scans to measure the **activation** of different areas during exercise. Peter's **therapist** designed a programme of repetitive movements and speech games. \"Every **tactic** is about repetition,\" she explains.\n\nThe centre is designed for **accessibility**, with wide doors, ramps and good **ventilation** in every room. An **administrator** told me that staff shortages are a constant challenge; a hospital **recruiter** visits universities every year looking for nurses. One young **staffer** said she chose the job because opportunities to help people **abound** here.\n\nPatients come from many backgrounds. One woman from a **tribal** community in Canada uses traditional drumming in her therapy. Another man, a former worker at a **rum** factory in Jamaica, practises writing by copying old recipes. Simple foods are a **staple** of the menu, and patients are encouraged to cook together.\n\nRecovery is not always easy. Peter's family received plenty of **unsolicited** advice from friends, and they had to cope with the **withdrawal** of some government support. His **adviser** at the centre and his personal **advisor** from his insurance company both helped with the paperwork. Today, his wife says she can see the old **radiance** in his face again. \"He's back,\" she says simply."},"29":{"w":["advocacy","affirmative","affordability","agreeableness","amiable","animate","anymore","appal","arguably","attainable","attendee","baseline","bio","biologically","borrower","chunk","collector","commemorate","communicator","connotation"],"title":"Can We Afford a Home?","t":"Last month, more than five hundred people gathered in Toronto for a conference on housing **affordability**. Each **attendee** received a printed programme with a short **bio** of every speaker. The atmosphere was friendly, but the topic was serious: in many cities, owning a home is no longer **attainable** for young people.\n\nThe opening speaker, an **amiable** economist with a talent for storytelling, was an excellent **communicator**. She used animated graphs to **animate** her talk, showing how house prices have risen compared with wages. Using 1990 as a **baseline**, prices have tripled, while incomes have barely grown. \"This should **appal** all of us,\" she said.\n\nExperts discussed possible solutions. One group focused on **advocacy** for social housing, while others proposed helping first-time **borrowers|borrower** with lower interest rates. A data **collector** from the city council presented statistics on empty flats owned by investors. Some speakers called for **affirmative** action to help families who have faced discrimination in the housing market.\n\nPsychologists also took part. One explained that people high in **agreeableness** tend to avoid conflict with landlords, even when they are treated unfairly. Another argued that humans are **biologically** wired to seek safety and stability, which is why insecure housing causes so much stress. The word \"home\", she noted, has a powerful **connotation** of belonging.\n\n**Arguably**, the most moving moment came at the end, when the conference paused to **commemorate** a housing activist who had died the previous year. A large **chunk** of the audience stood to applaud her work. Many attendees said they did not feel hopeless **anymore**."},"30":{"w":["credibility","cultivation","deadlock","defensive","deplorable","deplore","desolation","disbelieve","discriminate","doctoral","donation","duplication","eagerness","effortful","excerpt","footwear","foreseeable","forestry","futile","governmental"],"title":"Rebuilding a Burned Forest","t":"Three years ago, a wildfire swept through a large forest in southern Australia. When the smoke cleared, the scene was one of total **desolation**. Thousands of hectares of trees had been destroyed, and at first many residents seemed to **disbelieve** their own eyes; they had never imagined such damage was possible.\n\nRecovery has been slow and **effortful**. Experts in **forestry** have worked with local volunteers to plant new trees. A **doctoral** student, Emma Clarke, is studying which species grow best in burned soil. She explains that scientists must **discriminate** carefully between native plants and invasive species. The **cultivation** of young trees in nurseries requires patience, and many seedlings die in hot summers.\n\nThe project depends on public **donations|donation** and **governmental** support. Negotiations over funding reached a **deadlock** last year, as different agencies argued about responsibility. Some officials became **defensive** when journalists asked why help was so slow. Environmental groups **deplore** the delays, describing conditions for local farmers as **deplorable**.\n\nTo maintain **credibility**, the project publishes all its data online, avoiding any **duplication** of effort between organisations. Its annual report includes an **excerpt** from the diary of a firefighter who fought the blaze. Volunteers wear heavy **footwear** to protect their feet from sharp, burned wood.\n\nSome critics say that planting trees is **futile** if the climate keeps warming. But Emma's **eagerness** remains strong. \"In the **foreseeable** future, fires will be more common,\" she says. \"That is exactly why we must learn how forests recover.\""},"31":{"w":["graphic","havoc","headphone","hearted","homeowner","horrify","hygiene","hypothesize","idealistic","impractical","inability","inadequacy","inappropriate","incidental","inclusion","incomprehensible","inconsistent","inconvenience","informative","inhibition"],"title":"When the Water Main Burst","t":"Last winter, a major water pipe burst under our street, causing **havoc** in the neighbourhood. Water flooded basements, roads were closed and hundreds of homes were left without running water for a week. For every **homeowner** on the street, it was a serious **inconvenience**.\n\nThe damage was shocking. **Graphic** photos of flooded kitchens appeared online, and they seemed to **horrify** people across the city. Without clean water, **hygiene** became a real concern, especially for families with young children. The council set up water stations, but many older residents had an **inability** to carry heavy bottles home.\n\nCommunication was a problem. Information from the council was **inconsistent**: one notice said repairs would take two days, another said ten. Some updates were full of technical jargon that was almost **incomprehensible**. Residents complained that the council's website was not **informative**, and one official made an **inappropriate** joke that angered many people.\n\nEngineers began to **hypothesize** about the cause. Most believe the pipe was simply too old, a sign of the **inadequacy** of the city's ageing infrastructure. Some residents proposed an **idealistic** plan to replace every pipe in the city at once, but officials said it was **impractical**. Additional **incidental** costs, such as hotel rooms for displaced families, also had to be paid.\n\nStill, the crisis brought out the best in people. **Kind-hearted|hearted** neighbours shared food and water, and children wore **headphones|headphone** to block out the noise of the repair machines while doing homework. Residents demanded **inclusion** in future planning decisions. \"We lost our **inhibition** about speaking up,\" one woman said. \"Now the council listens.\""},"32":{"w":["injustice","instinctive","instructive","intrusion","invasive","involuntary","irresponsible","isle","juvenile","knowledgeable","laborious","legendary","lifeless","lifelong","lure","magical","mainstream","marketable","mentality","methodology"],"title":"Saving the Birds of a Small Isle","t":"Far off the coast of Scotland lies a small **isle** that is home to thousands of seabirds. For decades, however, the birds were in danger. Rats, brought by ships in the nineteenth century, had become an **invasive** species, eating eggs and **juvenile** birds. Some parts of the island had become almost **lifeless**.\n\nFive years ago, conservationists began an ambitious project to remove the rats. The work was **laborious**: teams walked every metre of the island, placing traps with food to **lure** the animals. Their **methodology** was carefully planned and tested on other islands first. A **knowledgeable** local guide, who has studied birds all his life, helped them find the rats' hiding places.\n\nThe project raised difficult questions. Some animal rights groups described the killing of rats as an **injustice**, while others called the earlier introduction of rats an **irresponsible** human mistake that had to be corrected. Islanders worried about the **intrusion** of outsiders into their quiet community, though most supported the plan.\n\nThe results have been **magical**. Bird numbers have doubled, and the island's **legendary** puffin colonies are returning. Watching the birds, visitors often feel an **involuntary** smile spread across their faces. Seabirds return to the same nesting places every year, an **instinctive** behaviour that scientists still do not fully understand.\n\nThe project has been **instructive** for other conservation efforts. Eco-tourism has made the island's birds a **marketable** attraction, bringing income to residents. Conservation, once a minority interest, has become **mainstream**. For the guide, it is the reward for a **lifelong** passion. \"We need a new **mentality**,\" he says. \"We broke nature. Now we have to help fix it.\""},"33":{"w":["misguided","misrepresent","motivational","newborn","nutritionist","observatory","odds","organizer","outfit","outrageous","outreach","override","oversee","overturn","overuse","pathway","persuasive","phd","playful","poise"],"title":"A Night at the Observatory","t":"Every autumn, the university **observatory** opens its doors to the public for one night. This year's **organizer** was Mei Lin, a **PhD** student in astronomy. Her goal was simple: to show young people that science is exciting and open to everyone.\n\nThe event was part of the university's **outreach** programme. Mei gave a **motivational** talk about her own **pathway** into science, describing how she was the first person in her family to attend university. She spoke with great **poise**, and her **persuasive** enthusiasm inspired many of the teenagers in the audience. Several said afterwards that they wanted to study physics.\n\nThe highlight was the telescope. Visitors could see craters on the Moon and clouds of gas where **newborn** stars are forming. Mei explained that the **odds** of seeing a shooting star that night were high, and the crowd cheered when one appeared. She also warned that the **overuse** of street lights makes it harder to see the sky. Small children, wearing a warm **outfit** and woolly hats, were especially **playful**, running between telescopes.\n\nMei also challenged some common myths. She said that the media often **misrepresent** astronomy, making **outrageous** claims about asteroids destroying Earth. Many people hold the **misguided** belief that star signs control their lives. \"Science sometimes has to **overturn** popular ideas,\" she said, \"and evidence must **override** our wishes.\"\n\nProfessors helped **oversee** the event, and a **nutritionist** from the medical school brought healthy snacks for volunteers. By midnight, hundreds of visitors had come and gone. Mei looked tired but happy. \"If one child goes home curious,\" she said, \"it was worth it.\""},"34":{"w":["popularize","portrayal","positivity","potent","prehistoric","progression","protector","provider","redefine","renewal","renovate","resentful","retrain","revival","revolutionize","rigor","ruinous","sameness","sensational","sensory"],"title":"A Museum Reborn","t":"For years, the city's natural history museum was in decline. Its halls were dark, its displays were old, and visitor numbers were falling. Then a new director arrived with a bold plan to **renovate** the building and **redefine** what a museum could be.\n\nThe director wanted to **revolutionize** the visitor experience. Instead of rows of glass cases, the new museum offers **sensory** displays: visitors can touch fossils, hear the sounds of **prehistoric** animals and walk through a model of an ancient forest. A technology **provider** created digital screens that show the **progression** of life on Earth over millions of years.\n\nThe project also aimed to **popularize** science without losing scientific **rigor**. Experts checked every display carefully. The museum's **portrayal** of early humans was updated to reflect recent research, replacing old images that were inaccurate. Critics who expected **sensational**, theme-park-style entertainment were pleasantly surprised.\n\nChange was not easy. The renovation was expensive, and some councillors warned that the costs could be **ruinous**. A few long-serving staff were **resentful** about the new approach, and the museum had to **retrain** many employees to use new technology. The director, however, remained full of **positivity**, describing the project as a \"**renewal** of the museum's mission\".\n\nThe results speak for themselves. Visitor numbers have tripled, and the museum has become a **potent** symbol of the city's cultural **revival**. Local schools describe it as a **protector** of curiosity. \"Museums must avoid **sameness**,\" the director says. \"Every visit should feel like a discovery.\""},"35":{"w":["signaling","someday","sparingly","speculation","speedily","starter","stimulation","subtlety","surgical","susceptibility","tactfully","telemedicine","toll","tonne","tranquility","transformative","trendy","trillion","unacceptable","unanticipated"],"title":"The Doctor on the Screen","t":"In remote parts of Canada, the nearest hospital can be hundreds of kilometres away. For years, patients had to travel for hours to see a specialist. Today, **telemedicine** is changing that. Doctors can examine patients by video, review test results online and even guide nurses through minor **surgical** procedures.\n\nThe change has been **transformative**. A young mother in a mountain village can now speak to a doctor within minutes instead of driving for a day. Prescriptions can be renewed **speedily**, and doctors can monitor patients with a high **susceptibility** to infection. Many older patients also value the **tranquility** of being treated at home. The **toll** of long journeys, especially in winter, has been greatly reduced.\n\nNot everything is simple. Some symptoms are hard to see on a screen, and doctors must learn to notice every **subtlety** in a patient's voice or expression. They must also deliver bad news **tactfully** without being in the same room. Internet connections in some areas are slow, and **unanticipated** technical problems can interrupt appointments.\n\nThe economic stakes are enormous. Global health spending exceeds ten **trillion** dollars a year, and telemedicine could reduce costs significantly. Investors have rushed into the market, and there is much **speculation** about which companies will succeed. Some critics warn that the trend is becoming too **trendy**, and that cutting traditional services would be **unacceptable**.\n\nResources remain limited and must be used **sparingly**. An emergency helicopter flight, one official noted, costs as much as a **tonne** of basic medicine, so every case that can be handled by video saves money for those who truly need to travel.\n\nFor doctors, the work offers new forms of professional **stimulation**. One nurse described rural telemedicine as an ideal **starter** job for young doctors. Researchers are even studying how the brain's **signaling** changes during long screen sessions, to help staff avoid fatigue. \"**Someday**,\" one doctor said, \"this will simply be normal.\""},"36":{"w":["uncommon","undeniably","underlie","underprivileged","undervalue","underway","undisguised","unequal","unhappiness","unheard","unhelpful","unintended","unrestricted","unskilled","unwelcome","upcoming","usable","venue","verification","wary"],"title":"A Festival for Everyone","t":"Every summer, a free music festival takes place in a park in one of the poorest districts of London. It was founded ten years ago to give **underprivileged** young people a chance to perform and enjoy live music. Preparations for this year's **upcoming** event are already **underway**.\n\nThe festival's organisers believe that talent is not **uncommon** in poor neighbourhoods; it is simply **undervalued|undervalue**. Many young musicians have never performed in public because they lack money, equipment or a suitable **venue**. Their voices, the organisers say, often go **unheard**. The festival offers **unrestricted** entry and free instruments, and even **unskilled** volunteers are trained to help.\n\nThe idea has not always been easy to put into practice. In the early years, some neighbours were **wary**, worried about noise and crowds. A few officials were **unhelpful**, and the council once rejected the plan because there was no **usable** space. Organisers also had to introduce ticket **verification** after the festival's popularity led to the **unintended** problem of overcrowding.\n\nThe festival highlights a deeper issue. Access to culture in the city remains **unequal**, and researchers say this inequality may **underlie** much of the **unhappiness** that young people in poor areas report. For many teenagers, music offers both an escape and a way to express themselves. Some performers have gone on to sign recording contracts.\n\nOn festival day, the atmosphere is **undeniably** joyful. Children dance with **undisguised** excitement, and grandparents sit on blankets listening to new bands. Even the loudest music is no longer **unwelcome**; most neighbours now attend themselves. \"This park belongs to all of us,\" one organiser said."},"37":{"w":["waterway","wholesale","workload","worrisome","wow","adorable","affiliation","airtime","amnesia","ancestry","anew","antagonism","aptitude","arbitrate","archivist","artery","aspirant","authorship","beholder","benefaction"],"title":"The Archivist and the Lost Letters","t":"In the city of Bruges, where every canal and **waterway** once served as a trade **artery**, an **archivist** named Anna Peeters made a remarkable discovery last year. While sorting boxes in the city archive, she found a bundle of letters written by a young poet in the 1850s. The question of their **authorship** soon caused excitement across Belgium.\n\nAnna has a natural **aptitude** for old handwriting, but the work was hard. Her normal **workload** was already heavy, and the letters were badly damaged. She discovered that they had been written by an unknown **aspirant** poet to a wealthy merchant who ran a **wholesale** wine business. The merchant's **benefaction** had funded the poet's studies.\n\nThe letters revealed a story of love and conflict. The poet wrote about the **antagonism** between his family and the merchant's family, and about a court case that a judge had been asked to **arbitrate**. Some passages were so beautiful that Anna simply said \"**Wow**\" aloud when she read them.\n\nThe discovery had a personal side too. An elderly man contacted the archive, believing the poet was part of his **ancestry**. He had been suffering from partial **amnesia** after an illness, and reading the letters helped him recall stories his grandmother once told. \"I feel as if I have found my family **anew**,\" he said.\n\nThe story received wide radio **airtime**, and the archive has strengthened its **affiliation** with a local university. Some experts find the slow pace of research **worrisome**, warning that many old documents are decaying. Anna, however, is optimistic. Among the letters, she found an **adorable** drawing of a cat, signed by the poet. \"Beauty,\" she says, \"is in the eye of the **beholder**.\""},"38":{"w":["botanist","carer","causation","charitable","childbirth","chromosome","citizenship","clarity","clench","collectivism","complacent","confide","conservatism","consumerism","contemplation","contentious","convergence","conveyor","counterfeit","crackdown"],"title":"A Botanist's Quiet Revolution","t":"Dr. Rosa Klein is a **botanist** who studies the genetics of wild plants. In her small laboratory, she counts the **chromosome** pairs of rare flowers, searching for clues about how plants adapt to climate change. Her work has gained international attention, but she remains modest.\n\nRosa's research raises important questions of **causation**. Do plants change because of rising temperatures, or because of other factors such as pollution? She insists on **clarity** in her conclusions, warning scientists not to become **complacent**. Her findings have shown a **convergence** of evidence: many species are moving to higher altitudes as the climate warms.\n\nHer career has not followed a typical path. For years, she worked part-time while serving as the main **carer** for her elderly mother. She often **confided|confide** to friends that she felt torn between duty and ambition. Rosa was born abroad and gained **citizenship** only after university. She sometimes jokes that she learned patience from plants and from her mother, a midwife who helped with **childbirth** in rural villages for forty years.\n\nRosa's work has a political side. She has criticised **counterfeit** herbal products that falsely claim to contain rare plants, and she supported a government **crackdown** on illegal plant trade. The issue is **contentious**; some traders say the rules are too strict. She also criticises modern **consumerism**, which she believes encourages people to buy without thinking.\n\nRosa values **contemplation**. She walks in her garden each morning, comparing the slow pace of nature with the speed of factory **conveyor** belts. She sees value in both **collectivism** and individual freedom, and in both **conservatism** and change. She donates part of her income to **charitable** causes. Asked about her greatest fear, she **clenched|clench** her hands. \"That we will notice too late,\" she said."},"39":{"w":["cult","cyborg","deforest","defuse","delusional","directness","discomfort","discredit","discreet","dishearten","droplet","earner","echolocation","effortlessly","electrification","embodiment","encyclopedia","enhancement","enrage","envoy"],"title":"What Bats Can Teach Us","t":"Bats are among the most misunderstood animals on Earth. Many people associate them with darkness and horror films, and some bat films have even developed a **cult** following. Yet scientists say bats are extraordinary creatures, and they may inspire new technology.\n\nMost bats find their way using **echolocation**. They make high-pitched sounds and listen to the echoes, building a picture of their surroundings. They can catch insects **effortlessly** in complete darkness and even detect a falling water **droplet**. Engineers study this ability to improve sensors for cars and robots, and some researchers imagine a future **cyborg** that uses similar technology as a sensory **enhancement** for blind people.\n\nBats face serious threats. Many species are losing their homes as companies **deforest** large areas for farming. The rapid **electrification** of rural areas has also brought bright lights that confuse them. Last year, a United Nations **envoy** described bats as \"the forgotten **embodiment** of biodiversity\" and called for stronger protection.\n\nPublic attitudes are a challenge. Some people hold **delusional** beliefs that bats attack humans, and false stories online can quickly **discredit** conservation groups. Scientists try to **defuse** fear through education, speaking with **directness** but also kindness. Some people still feel **discomfort** at the sight of a bat, and cruel treatment of the animals can **enrage** volunteers.\n\nConservationists remain hopeful. Farmers have begun to value bats as natural pest control, which saves money for every **earner** in rural communities. A small, **discreet** bat box in a garden can provide a safe home. \"Don't let fear **dishearten** you,\" one biologist says. \"Look them up in any **encyclopedia**, and you'll see how amazing they are.\""},"40":{"w":["epic","erasure","existential","expectant","fantasize","fascist","feebleness","femininity","fertility","finder","footprint","forgo","frantic","frugality","gassy","geographic","geological","grader","gravitate","groundnut"],"title":"Grandmother's Epic Story","t":"My great-grandmother Lucia turned one hundred last spring. Her life has been an **epic** journey across a century of change. She grew up in a small village in southern Italy, where her family grew wheat and **groundnut** crops on land whose **fertility** depended on the rains.\n\nAs a young woman, Lucia lived through the rise of a **fascist** government and the war that followed. Food was scarce, and **frugality** was a way of life. She learned to **forgo** almost everything except bread and vegetables. When she became an **expectant** mother in 1945, she walked ten kilometres to the nearest doctor. \"I never had time to **fantasize** about an easy life,\" she says.\n\nAfter the war, the family emigrated to Canada. Lucia remembers feeling **frantic** on the ship, worried about her children. She found work in a factory and later opened a small bakery. In the 1950s, many people held narrow ideas about **femininity**, but Lucia ran her business alone and proudly.\n\nToday, Lucia faces the **feebleness** of old age with humour. She sometimes asks big, **existential** questions about life and memory, and she worries about the **erasure** of family history. Last year, my cousin, a fifth **grader**, became the **finder** of her old diary in the attic. The children always **gravitate** to her stories, and her diary is now being typed up for the family.\n\nLucia still offers advice. She urges us to reduce our carbon **footprint** by wasting less, and she dislikes **gassy** soft drinks, preferring water. My uncle, a **geological** engineer, once tried to explain the **geographic** features of her home region using maps. She listened patiently, then laughed. \"I know that land,\" she said. \"I carried it on my shoes.\""}});
